00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.640
most of us just love a good film halation,&nbsp;
but it s pretty hard to understand how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.860
to create the effect in such a way that is&nbsp;
rendering a natural and subtle halation bleeding

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.220
so in today s video I will show you exactly how I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:34.320
cracked the best halation settings and how to&nbsp;
understand what each slider is actually doing

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.000
before we start, it took me quite some&nbsp;
time experimenting and testing hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.240
of shoots to find these settings, so&nbsp;
why not hit the like button and share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.720
the video with other people that&nbsp;
might need this in their workflow.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.480
No more time wasting, let s get into davinci&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:55.740
Enter color tab by either pressing on it or&nbsp;
hitting hotkey shift 4 on windows, or cmd 4 on mac&nbsp;

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:59.880
In the upright corner hit the&nbsp;
effects menu and search for halation&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:06.120
drag it on top of the desired node and let s start&nbsp;
analyzing the first options we ll be working with

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.180
first thing, we have color space processing
you either leave it to your project timeline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.040
input, or you can open and choose the&nbsp;
type of color space you have shot in

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.060
This particular video was shot on the&nbsp;
Cinechrome Sony Film Simulation Recipe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.760
which uses a bt2020 color space, so&nbsp;
i ll just go ahead and choose bt2020&nbsp;

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.320
These sony film simulations are based off the&nbsp;
picture profile menu so if you are interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.580
n film simulations for sony cameras, go check&nbsp;
my website for the picture profile settings

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.420
back to the halation menu
You can leave the isolation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.980
and the dye layer as they are for the moment,&nbsp;
and let s jump directly to the secondary glow

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.440
I will guide you through a couple adjustments&nbsp;
and afterwards I will explain what and why

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.020
bring up the strength&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:59.160
and lift the gamma all the way to the top
as you can see the halation is already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.940
showing very strongly through the&nbsp;
image, so bring down the spread

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.360
it looks better, but the&nbsp;
colors are still very vibrant&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.320
the main problem here, the&nbsp;
secondary glow color is bright red,&nbsp;

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.000
Let s change the color of the&nbsp;
filter to a dark brown tone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.460
and that will solve most of our problems

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.980
so as a basic explanation, halation&nbsp;
takes place in areas of high contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:31.680
usually between sky and a dark shadow
so what i ve done here, is i ve boosted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.420
the power and the contrast of the secondary&nbsp;
glow to make sure the halation will show up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:42.840
even in areas of lower contrast, like between&nbsp;
branches, and reduced the spread to a minimum so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:48.780
it s not bleeding all over, making it present&nbsp;
over the whole image but in very subtle way

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.640
I ve changed the initial color of&nbsp;
the halation which was very bright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.800
and vibrant to a darker shade of orange&nbsp;
and that is meant to solve the color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.700
brightness problem while also giving&nbsp;
a natural toned down halation effect

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.100
it s looking great already

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.340
some people like this kind of&nbsp;
look and they can stop here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:13.920
but for more natural and organic look let&nbsp;
s continue with a couple minor adjustments

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.300
In the dye layer reflection
bring down the strength just by a pinch&nbsp;

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.620
and you can bring up the gamma to increase&nbsp;
halation in areas of lower contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.060
but that s your choice

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.440
you can also adjust the spread&nbsp;
level even more to reduce or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.240
increase the bleeding of the red halation effect&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.800
and there is also relative spread&nbsp;
which can be shown by checking this box

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.140
here you can balance out individual&nbsp;
color channels Red green and blue&nbsp;

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.860
favouring one over the other&nbsp;
will make it more dominant

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:45.780
we ll leave this for another video

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.400
next and final steps
in the isolation submenu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:56.700
set the threshold at your desired level, each clip&nbsp;
will require a different threshold level since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.860
each clip will have a different highlight point
so adjust it to your own liking and depending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.680
how much of the image you want&nbsp;
affected by the red halation

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.660
lastly film saturation, this&nbsp;
is again your personal choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:16.200
but I would recommend going for&nbsp;
something subtle from 0.10 to 0.35

